---
// DocsEnvReference: Complete Environment Variables (.env) reference table
---

<h2 id="env-reference">
  Environment Variables Reference (<code>.env</code>)
</h2>
<div class="table-wrapper">
  <table class="comparison-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Variable</th>
        <th>Default</th>
        <th>Description</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>ACCESS_TOKEN</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <em>Required</em>
        </td>
        <td>
          GitHub Personal Access Token (classic with <code>repo</code> scope).
          For Actions minutes panel, token must also have billing-read access
          for target owner/org.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>OWNER</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <em>Optional</em>
        </td>
        <td>
          GitHub username to monitor for auto-discovery. Set one of{" "}
          <code>OWNER</code>, <code>ORG</code> or <code>REPOS</code>.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>RUNNER_BACKEND</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>auto</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          Execution engine: <code>auto</code>, <code>docker</code>,{" "}
          <code>orbstack-vm</code>, <code>wsl2</code>, <code>multipass</code>.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>AUTO_ROUTE_VM</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>true</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          Route jobs to a VM when a <code>VM_TRIGGER_LABELS</code> word appears
          in their labels, job name or workflow name, or when their steps need
          more than a container runner: <code>services:</code> or a
          <code>container:</code>, a <code>docker://</code> action,
          <code>docker run</code> with mounts or published ports,
          <code>docker compose up</code>, kind/minikube/k3d, or
          <code>systemctl</code>.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>RUNNER_ARCH</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>both</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          Target architecture: <code>both</code> (ARM64 + AMD64),{" "}
          <code>arm64</code>, <code>amd64</code>. With <code>both</code>, a
          job's arch label decides, and an unlabelled job runs natively on the
          host's arch.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>ACTIVE_REPO_DAYS</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>60</code>
        </td>
        <td>Only monitor repositories with commits in the last N days.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>MAX_RUNNERS</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>4</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          Maximum concurrent runner instances allowed on this workstation.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>RUNNER_CPUS / RUNNER_MEMORY</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <em>derived</em>
        </td>
        <td>
          Per-runner CPU and memory limit for Docker containers, OrbStack job
          VMs (set on every clone) and Multipass VMs. Unset, each runner gets an
          equal share of the host (OrbStack: its VM pool) after keeping 1 CPU
          and 2 GiB back: <code>(cpus - 1) // MAX_RUNNERS</code>. The startup
          log and the dashboard show the sizing and warn when{" "}
          <code>MAX_RUNNERS</code> &times; the limit exceeds the host.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>RUNNER_SIZING</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>auto</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>unlimited</code> restores the old behavior: no limits, every
          runner may claim every host core.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>CLEANUP_RUNNERS_ON_SHUTDOWN</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>false</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          Destroy runners when the autoscaler stops. Off by default, so
          restarting or redeploying the autoscaler no longer cancels jobs that
          are running; runners are ephemeral and exit after their job.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>NATIVE_ARCH_OVERRIDE</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>off</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>off</code>, <code>all</code>, or comma-separated{" "}
          <code>owner/repo</code> names. On Apple Silicon, serves those
          repositories' <code>amd64</code>-labelled jobs with native arm64
          runners, without changing the workflow. See{" "}
          <a href="#native-arch-override">Native-arch override</a>.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>PROXIES_ENABLED</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>true</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          Start apt-cacher, Verdaccio, Athens, and Docker Hub caching proxies.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>RATE_LIMIT_REFRESH_INTERVAL</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>60</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          Polling interval (seconds) for GitHub API quota telemetry updates.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>ACTIONS_BILLING_REFRESH_INTERVAL</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>300</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          Polling interval (seconds) for GitHub Actions minutes billing updates.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>GITLAB_URL</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>https://gitlab.com</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          Base URL for GitLab API. Can be set to any private self-hosted GitLab
          instance (e.g. <code>https://git.eckelmann.de</code>).
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>GITLAB_TOKEN</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <em>Optional</em>
        </td>
        <td>
          GitLab Personal Access Token, Group Access Token, or Project Access
          Token with <code>api</code> scope for discovering runners and job
          queues.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>GITLAB_GROUP</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <em>Optional</em>
        </td>
        <td>
          GitLab group to discover and monitor runners and pending pipeline
          jobs.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>GITLAB_PROJECTS</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <em>Optional</em>
        </td>
        <td>
          Comma-separated list of GitLab project paths to monitor (e.g.{" "}
          <code>group/project</code>).
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>PROVIDERS_CONFIG</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <em>Optional</em>
        </td>
        <td>
          Path to a multi-instance providers YAML configuration file for
          enterprise setups.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>AUTO_DISCOVER_REPOS</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>true</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          Discover and monitor every recently active repository of{" "}
          <code>OWNER</code> or <code>ORG</code>.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>CACHE_ENABLED</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>true</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          Mount persistent package and tool caches from{" "}
          <code>HOST_CACHE_DIR</code> into runners.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>DASHBOARD_ENABLED</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>true</code>
        </td>
        <td>Serve the dashboard and control-plane API.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>DASHBOARD_HOST</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>127.0.0.1</code>
        </td>
        <td>Dashboard listen address.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>DASHBOARD_PORT</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>49505</code>
        </td>
        <td>Dashboard listen port.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>DISCOVERY_INTERVAL</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>900</code>
        </td>
        <td>How often (seconds) repository auto-discovery runs.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <em>Optional</em>
        </td>
        <td>
          Fallback for <code>ACCESS_TOKEN</code> when that is unset.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>HOST_CACHE_DIR</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <em>Optional</em>
        </td>
        <td>
          Host directory holding the persistent per-runner caches; caching is
          off when empty.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>MIN_RUNNERS</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>0</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          Warm standby pool: idle runners kept ready after queued jobs are
          served (org scope in <code>ORG</code> mode, else round-robin across
          repos by priority; capped by <code>MAX_RUNNERS</code>; exempt from
          idle reaping).
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>MULTIPASS_IMAGE</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>24.04</code>
        </td>
        <td>Ubuntu image Multipass launches per job.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>ORG</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <em>Optional</em>
        </td>
        <td>
          Organization mode: runners register at org scope and serve the org's
          active repositories.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>POLL_INTERVAL</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>10</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          Queue check interval in seconds; stretched automatically as the GitHub
          API quota runs low.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>REPO</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <em>Optional</em>
        </td>
        <td>
          Fallback for <code>REPOS</code> when that is unset.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>REPOS</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <em>Optional</em>
        </td>
        <td>
          Comma-separated <code>owner/repo</code> list to monitor instead of
          auto-discovery.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>REPO_PRIORITY</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <em>Optional</em>
        </td>
        <td>Comma-separated repositories to serve first, in order.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>RUNNER_BUSY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>7200</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          Reap a runner GitHub has reported busy for longer than this (stuck
          job).
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>VM_BACKEND</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>auto</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          VM engine for VM-routed jobs: <code>auto</code> (OrbStack, or the
          engine <code>RUNNER_BACKEND</code> names), <code>orbstack</code>,{" "}
          <code>wsl2</code> or <code>multipass</code>.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>VM_TRIGGER_LABELS</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>vm,browser,e2e,…</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          Words that route a job to a VM when found in its labels, job name or
          workflow name.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>WSL_BASE_TARBALL</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <em>Optional</em>
        </td>
        <td>
          Root filesystem tarball imported as a fresh WSL2 distro per job,
          unregistered after the job (recommended).
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>WSL_DISTRO_BASE</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <code>Ubuntu-24.04</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          WSL2 distro that runs jobs when no <code>WSL_BASE_TARBALL</code> is
          set (needs a <code>runner</code> user with passwordless sudo).
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>WSL_RUNNER_DIR</code>
        </td>
        <td>
          <em>Optional</em>
        </td>
        <td>
          Where per-job WSL2 distros are installed (default: the state
          directory).
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
